{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's 'negative heuristic' fails to distinguish between a theory that has survived severe tests and one that has simply not been tested at all. If preference is based solely on the absence of falsification among known alternatives, an untested conjecture would be equally preferable to a severely corroborated one, provided neither has been falsified. This contradicts the prompt's premise that scientists (and Popper) specifically prefer the *better-corroborated* theory, implying that surviving severe tests confers a specific status distinct from mere survival or lack of testing. The target's mechanism dissolves the role of 'severity' in corroboration, rendering the preference irrational under Popper's own emphasis on severe testing as the criterion for scientific progress.","premise":"Popper explicitly distinguishes between theories that have survived severe tests and those that have not, assigning greater 'corroboration' to the former, whereas the target's negative heuristic treats any non-falsified theory as equally actionable if no better alternative exists.","premises_essential":["c5385f48a8c1171e012d4f1382b3b7c2a9f55d0e55597c93063ddfaa5c16fa2f"],"successor_question":"How does the target's negative heuristic account for Popper's explicit insistence that corroboration reports must include details of the severity of tests undertaken, if severity adds no actionable value over mere non-falsification?","target":"c5385f48a8c1171e012d4f1382b3b7c2a9f55d0e55597c93063ddfaa5c16fa2f"}]}